# Runtime state — rewritten by the bot on essentially every tick, so they
# show up permanently dirty in `git status` and every commit picks up an
# unrelated diff. None of it is authored; all of it is regenerated (or
# starts empty) on a fresh checkout.
#
# NOT ignored: data/skills/*.json. Those look like churn too — save_all()
# rewrites last_used/success_count on every shutdown — but the mine_*,
# dig_up, chop_tree, ... files are hand-authored skill definitions
# (see skills/skill_system.py, _dedupe's "hand-authored skill" guard) and
# losing them from the repo would lose real content. The stat fields
# living in the same file as the definition is the actual problem there.
data/attention_focus.json
data/goals.json
data/hardware_manifest.json
data/last_train.json
data/progression.json
data/q_table.json
data/supervisor_audit.jsonl
data/world_memory.json
data/world_model.json
data/memory/episodes.jsonl
data/memory/retired_goals.jsonl
data/memory/skill_evolution.jsonl

# Secrets — the Telegram bot token lives at ~/.config/telegram/token,
# deliberately outside this tree. These patterns only exist so a stray
# copy inside the repo can't be committed by accident.
*.token
.token
token
telegram_token*
.env
